The previous script failed because Android stores the actual .apk file one level deeper inside another subfolder, rather than directly inside the ~~ directories.
We can use aapt directly to read the package names instead of using strings.
## Step 1: Run the Fixed Find Script
Run this single command from your current directory. It accurately handles the subfolder layer and uses aapt to give you the clean package names:

find . -name "base.apk" -exec sh -c 'echo "File: {}"; aapt dump badging "{}" | grep "package: name=" | head -n 1' \;

## Step 2: Read the Output
The output will clearly map the file paths to the actual app names like this:

File: ./~~Ci9QE5lV-OrNE42v23iq6Q==/com.example.app-someid==/base.apk
package: name='com.example.app' versionCode='1' versionName='1.0'

## Step 3: Copy Your Target APK
Once you find your app in the list, copy it straight to your Downloads folder using the full path shown in the script output:

cp ./~~FOLDER_NAME_HERE==/SUB_FOLDER_HERE/base.apk ~/Downloads/my_test_app.apk

